 * @file README.dox
 *  
 * Introduction
 * 
 * The goal of this project is to work with strings from files and search for a smaller DNA pattern 
 * inside a larger DNA sequence using two different algorithms.
 *
 * Program Description
 *
 * The program is called patternMatching.c.  
 * It reads two text files from the command line:
 * - one file with a DNA sequence
 * - one file with the pattern to search for
 *
 * The program then finds how many times the pattern appears in the DNA sequence.
 *
 * Algorithms
 *
 * The user can choose one of two algorithms to make the search of the pattern in the DNA string .
 * The two algorithms are:
 * -Brute Force (-bf): checks every position in the DNA string one by one.
 * -Karp–Rabin (-kr):  uses hashing to make the search faster.
 *
 *  How to Run
 *
 * The program runs from the terminal like this:
 *
 * ./patternMatching -bf dnaSequence.txt patSequence.txt 
 * ./patternMatching -kr swinefluDNA.txt normal-dna.txt
 *
 *
 * Example output:
 * 
 * The pattern was found: 53 times
 * 
 *
 * Input Format
 *
 * Each file has one line with the DNA sequence or pattern (only a, c, g, t).  
 * The maximum allowed size is 512000 characters.
 *
 * Error Messages
 *
 * The program should print messages that warn that :
 * - DNA sequence too large
 * - Pattern sequence too large
 * - Error while opening file
 * - Wrong algorithm is given
 * - Wrong amount of arguments given from the command line
 * - Illegal character in input file
 * - Pattern is larger than the sequence
 */
